  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/7/74/Strange_Tales_Vol_1_122.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "A short recap of the events of [LINK:1963:FF_23|FF #23|2027-03-16] recounts how Dr. Doom dispensed with the three members of his \"sinister trio\" before being sent soaring off into space himself.&nbsp; This resulted in the loss of his power to keep his three henchmen in the dimension into which they had been banished, and by the end of page 3, they're shown discussing whether continuing to serve Doom is better or worse than going out on their own.\n\nThey decide in the end to try and show that they still have something to offer Doom (whenever he returns) and conclude that going after the Fantastic Four one member at a time would help prove their value.&nbsp; They decide to start with the Human Torch.\n\nSince they know where the Storms live, they soon attack Johnny in his own garage, tying him up in asbestos rope and hauling him out to a trailer.&nbsp; Johnny can't break free, but he manages to generate enough smoke, which seeps out of the trailer, eventually bringing in the fire department to investigate, who free him from his bindings.&nbsp; Then he heads back to his house, where the evil trio are waiting for Sue to appear.\n\nA few pages of fairly mundane action ensue, with Johnny no longer taken by surprise at their appearance.&nbsp; One of them jumps into his souped-up sports car, and Johnny has to corral the intruder without damaging his pride and joy, but he finally does.\n\nThe three are rounded up.",
  "grade_one_liner": "Even Dr. Doom knew these guys weren't worth keeping around.\nThe longest part of the story is convincing yourself it might get better.",
  "subtitle": "Three Against the Torch, One Against the Reader",
  "review_text": "It is astounding that Stan Lee thought that the three bumbling fools who Dr. Doom used for his own purposes... [SPAN: severity=error; text=before summarily casting aside...] were worth a second look.&nbsp; I wonder if <i>any</i> readers were actually wondering what became of them in the five months since they first appeared.\n[ASSET: center | TerribleTrio | width=large]\n\nTheir discussion didn't convince me for a moment that they really wanted to serve Doom.&nbsp; In fact, if it had been <i>most</i> people, after returning from the other dimension, it would have made <i>far</i> more sense for them to lie low and [SPAN: severity=info; text=hope that Doom never thought about them again], but <b><i>no</i></b><i>...</i> somehow they still think Doom might have need of them again.\n\nThis is patently foolish, since Doom only <i>uses</i> people, and these three bozos show they didn't learn <i>anything</i> from their one encounter with the would-be tyrant.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Idiots!]\n\nThe story really doesn't begin until the bottom of page 4, with even the narration admitting to the reader that \"you've probably never read a longer introduction before!\"\n\n[HANG0] It then promises a \"reward...\"\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Let's just say that I'm still waiting...]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | BattleBegins | width=14%] Johnny is gullible enough to fall for yet <i>another</i> automobile-related trap (just like he did in [LINK:1963:FF_23|FF #23|2027-03-16]), and before long, finds himself in a largely perfunctory battle against the same trio that Doom used to get the other FF members out of the way so that he could focus on Reed.\n[BR]\n[BR] To point out that he was initially captured and hauled off (to artificially fill pages)...\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; text=...goes without saying...]\n\n[ASSET: right | SummoningWithSmoke | width=12%] Only after generating a lot of smoke to attract the local fire department to the trailer park where he is being held captive is the Human Torch finally rescued (also allowing the co-creators to... <i>uh</i>... <b>burn</b> through three more pages).\n[BR]\n[BR] By this time, the trio has returned to the Storm residence to lie in wait for the Invisible Girl -- their next target.\n[BR]\n[BR] Johnny is none too happy and makes a beeline back home...\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Cue battle scene... <b>take 2</b>!]\n[ASSET: center | ReaderRage | width=large]\n(Except that <i>this</i> time, the story has advanced far enough to be almost over, allowing Johnny to prevail.)\n\nThe final member of the evil trio is finally cornered in Johnny's own sports car in a feeble attempt to add a little spice to the close-out panels.\n\nThe [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved]... [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=one yawn at a time...]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | ff23blunder | width=28%] [ASSET: right | ff22blunder | width=13%] I imagine if you so much as opened this book to the splash page, you spotted the editorial blunder.\n[BR]\n[BR] On the cover, it advertises the \"<b>3 Against the Torch</b>!\" -- those whom \"you met in [LINK:1963:FF_23|FF #23|2027-03-16]\" ([SPAN: severity=success; text=which is <i>correct</i>]).&nbsp; Yet, on the splash page, it indicates (in <i>two</i> places) they appeared in [LINK:1963:FF_22|FF #22|2027-02-17] (which featured the Mole Man, [SPAN: severity=error; text=so <i>incorrect</i>]).\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=small; text=(It's just one more example that demonstrates how bringing in a little editorial help for Stan Lee's writing might have helped...)]\n\nI did at least [SPAN: severity=success; text=chuckle at the cover image of Doctor Doom,] complete with the qualifier that he \"does <i>not</i> appear in this story!&nbsp; We just felt like drawing his face!\" [DECODER_RING: This immediately reminds me of one of John Byrne's earliest FF covers, done just after he took over the book in the 1980s -- issue #238.&nbsp; If you've not seen it, look it up!]&nbsp; Yet, despite the issue's cover claim, one need only open the book to the splash page to find Dr. Doom himself (though admittedly only in flashback)!\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n[HANG0] When you pick up an issue, see its cover, and instantly think to yourself,\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=large; text=\"Oh <i>no</i>!&nbsp; Not <i>these</i> three buffoons again!\"]\n[BR] That should tell you something about the title's track record.\n\nThe evidence is all there, and one need only consult a short list of the lame cast of characters that the Human Torch has had to contend with over the last few months.&nbsp; In no particular order, they are:\n[HANG] * Acrobat\n[HANG] * Plantman\n[HANG] * Eel\n[HANG] * Painter\n[HANG] * Asbestos Man\n[HANG] * Paste Pot Pete\n[HANG] * Rabble Rouser\n\nEven his [LINK:1963:ST_107_torch0|ST #107] battle with the Sub-Mariner didn't impress, though admittedly, his encounter with Sandman ([LINK:1963:ST_115_torch1|ST #115torch|2027-01-26]) was okay, as were his early conflicts with the Wizard.\n\nEven so, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=there isn't a reader alive who could classify <i>any</i> of this as <b>essential reading</b>!]\n\nSeeing that this issue features a return of this trio only elicits a groan.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: They are referred to elsewhere as the \"Terrible Trio\" -- a name that <i>never</i> appears in this magazine, though it <i>does</i> on the cover of issue 129, published later this year.]\n[BR] Nothing about their first appearance hinted they had earned a second engagement.\n[BR] Are these three <b>idiots</b> really the best you can offer us this month, Stan?\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=For a Human Torch feature that is <i>already</i> scraping the bottom of the villain barrel, bringing back these three <b>morons</b> seems like an act of creative desperation!]\n\nThe blurb on the credits box that reads [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Rapidly written by Stan Lee\"] only further suggests that these solo Human Torch stories are little more than an afterthought... or... <i>dare I suggest</i>... [SPAN: severity=error; text=a thinly veiled money-grab?]\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=Sigh... who's the villain here, Stan?]\n\nIf I wasn't clear enough above, let me spell it out:\n[HANG] * These three characters are <i>lame</i>.\n[HANG] * I find them stupid, gullible, and completely full of themselves.\n[HANG] * I was unconvinced by their stated goal of needing to prove their worth to Dr. Doom.\n[HANG] * Their actions never once seemed clever enough to warrant 14 pages featuring their return.\n[HANG] * [SPAN: severity=warning; text=I hope we never see any of them <i>again</i>!]&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: Sadly, the aforementioned issue 129 awaits...]\n\n[ASSET: left | NauseatingEnding | width=11%] The story was so formulaic and not worth bothering with that, by the time I reached its final, gag-inducing panel, which showed the Human Torch talking directly to the readers (and <i>waving</i> no less), urging them to buy this month's issue of the <i>Fantastic Four</i> and to come back for more solo Torch action next month...\n[BR]\n[BR] ...my brain had been addled enough to escape the nausea that would have ordinarily ensued.\n\nIronically, the smartest character in the comic is the one <b>who never appears</b>, yet somehow understood when it was time to discard his [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=dimwitted trio of lackeys!]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Four hours later, I can only conclude that Dr. Doom made the right call.]",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] After the recap of [LINK:1963:FF_23|FF #23|2027-03-16], page 3 pauses for just a moment to tell readers that Dr. Doom <i>will</i> return in the upcoming FF Annual 2.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=I found this a nice plug for the upcoming annual.]"
  ],
  "highlight": "Fourteen pages spent featuring the Human Torch against Dr. Doom's three discarded henchmen only reinforced the notion that I was perfectly happy not knowing what became of them."
}